= Solution
{parent=1}

Use degree-indexed <Fourier partial sums> and <Fejér sums>, as in this paper. For a $2\pi$-periodic <continuous function>, put
$$
\widehat f_k=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(t)e^{-ikt}\,dt,\qquad s_n(f,x)=\sum_{k=-n}^n\widehat f_ke^{ikx},\qquad \sigma_n(f)=\frac1{n+1}\sum_{j=0}^ns_j(f).
$$
Equivalently,
$$
\sigma_n(f,x)=\sum_{|k|\le n}\left(1-\frac{|k|}{n+1}\right)\widehat f_ke^{ikx}.
$$
These are <trigonometric polynomials> of degree at most $n$. Some treatments index the <Fejér sum> by the number of terms rather than its degree; the degree convention here is why the formulas below contain $\sigma_{2n-1}$ and $\sigma_{n-1}$.

= 2
{parent=2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <trigonometric polynomial> $t_n(x)=\sum_{|k|\le n}c_ke^{ikx}$, <orthogonality> of the exponential <Fourier modes> gives $s_j(t_n)=t_n$ whenever $j\ge n$. Every term in the <de la Vallée Poussin sum> has such an index, so
$$
\boxed{v_n(t_n)=\frac1n\sum_{j=n}^{2n-1}t_n=t_n.}
$$
Here and below $n\ge1$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Subtracting the two initial averages of <Fourier partial sums> gives
$$
\boxed{v_n=2\sigma_{2n-1}-\sigma_{n-1}},
$$
because $2n\sigma_{2n-1}=\sum_{j=0}^{2n-1}s_j$ and $n\sigma_{n-1}=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}s_j$. The positive normalized <Fejér kernel> gives the <uniform-norm contraction of Fejér summation>, namely $\|\sigma_mf\|_\infty\le\|f\|_\infty$. The <triangle inequality> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\|v_nf\|_\infty\le2\|\sigma_{2n-1}f\|_\infty+\|\sigma_{n-1}f\|_\infty\le3\|f\|_\infty.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For any <trigonometric polynomial> $t_n$ of degree at most $n$, reproduction from (a) and the <operator norm> bound from (b) imply
$$
f-v_nf=(f-t_n)-v_n(f-t_n),\qquad \|f-v_nf\|_\infty\le4\|f-t_n\|_\infty.
$$
Taking the <infimum> over $t_n$ gives
$$
\boxed{\|f-v_nf\|_\infty\le4E_n(f)}.
$$
This is the <polynomial reproduction error bound> with $\|v_n\|\le3$; attaining the <best uniform approximation> is not needed for this inequality.

= 3
