= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume the <spline knot sequence> is ordered and its relevant support spans satisfy $t_{i+k}>t_i$. For distinct knots, the <divided difference> is a finite linear combination of evaluations, so it commutes with integration. With $u\in[a,b]$,
$$
\int_a^b(u-t)_+^{k-1}\,dt=\int_a^u(u-t)^{k-1}\,dt=\frac{(u-a)^k}{k}.
$$
Taking the order-$k$ <divided difference> in $u$ gives
$$
\boxed{\int_a^bM_i(t)\,dt=k[t_i,\ldots,t_{i+k}]\frac{(u-a)^k}{k}=1}.
$$
Indeed, the order-$k$ <divided difference> of a degree-$k$ <polynomial> is its <leading coefficient>, here $1/k$. This proves the <unit-integral normalization of a B-spline>. Admissible repeated knots follow by coalescing knots, with the usual one-sided endpoint conventions.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $N_{i,k}$ for the order-$k$ <B-spline>. At order one, $N_{i,1}=\mathbf1_{[t_i,t_{i+1})}$, and the finite sum is one on its basic knot interval. For larger order, the <Cox-de Boor recurrence> is
$$
N_{i,k}(t)=\frac{t-t_i}{t_{i+k-1}-t_i}N_{i,k-1}(t)+\frac{t_{i+k}-t}{t_{i+k}-t_{i+1}}N_{i+1,k-1}(t),
$$
with a zero-denominator term defined as zero. Sum over $i=1,\ldots,n$. For each interior lower-order index $j=2,\ldots,n$, the combined coefficient is
$$
\frac{t-t_j}{t_{j+k-1}-t_j}+\frac{t_{j+k-1}-t}{t_{j+k-1}-t_j}=1.
$$
The two unmatched lower-order terms have supports $[t_1,t_k]$ and $[t_{n+1},t_{n+k}]$, respectively, and vanish on the open basic interval $(t_k,t_{n+1})$. Induction therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{i=1}^nN_{i,k}(t)=1,\qquad t_k\le t\le t_{n+1}}.
$$
For simple knots and $k\ge2$, <continuity> extends the identity to both endpoints. In the order-one case and at admissible repeated endpoint knots, use the one-sided value from the basic interval. Extending the knot sequence in both directions also proves the <subpartition of unity for B-splines>, $0\le\sum_iN_i\le1$ everywhere: the finite sum is a subset of a nonnegative full partition. That weaker global inequality will be needed in Question 7(a).

= 7
{parent=Paper 69}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <orthogonal projection> condition is $(f-s^*,N_i)=0$ for all $i$. Since $M_i=kN_i/(t_{i+k}-t_i)$, it is equivalently
$$
Ga=b,\qquad G_{ij}=(M_i,N_j),\qquad b_i=(M_i,f).
$$
The <mixed-normalization spline Gram matrix> is invertible: $G=D^{-1}H$, where $D_{ii}=(t_{i+k}-t_i)/k>0$ and $H_{ij}=(N_i,N_j)$ is the positive-definite ordinary <Gram matrix> of the <basis>. Nonnegativity and the <unit-integral normalization of a B-spline> give $|b_i|\le\|f\|_\infty\int_0^1M_i=\|f\|_\infty$. The <subpartition of unity for B-splines> gives, on all of $[0,1]$,
$$
|s^*(t)|\le\sum_j|a_j|N_j(t)\le\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\|P_Sf\|_\infty\le\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\|b\|_{\ell^\infty}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\|f\|_\infty,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\|P_S\|_\infty\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}}.
$$
This is the <maximum-norm bound for spline projection>. Using a subpartition matters when the basic knot interval is a proper subinterval of $[0,1]$. As in the paper's assertion that the image lies in $C[0,1]$, the <spline> space here must consist of <continuous functions>; order-one step splines or full-multiplicity interior knots do not have that property.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For distinct ordered knots, put $h_i=t_{i+1}-t_i>0$. The linear <B-spline> $N_i$ is the triangular hat supported on $[t_i,t_{i+2}]$, equal to $(t-t_i)/h_i$ on its rising segment and $(t_{i+2}-t)/h_{i+1}$ on its falling segment. Its integral-normalized partner is $M_i=2N_i/(h_i+h_{i+1})$. Thus
$$
\int N_i^2=\frac{h_i+h_{i+1}}3,\qquad \int N_iN_{i-1}=\frac{h_i}6,\qquad \int N_iN_{i+1}=\frac{h_{i+1}}6.
$$
For example, the left overlap is $\int_0^{h_i}(u/h_i)(1-u/h_i)\,du=h_i/6$. Nonadjacent hats have disjoint interiors of their <supports>, so their <inner product> is zero. The <linear-spline mixed Gram matrix> therefore has row
$$
\boxed{g_{ii}=\frac23,\quad g_{i,i-1}=\frac{h_i}{3(h_i+h_{i+1})},\quad g_{i,i+1}=\frac{h_{i+1}}{3(h_i+h_{i+1})},\quad g_{ij}=0\ (|i-j|\ge2)}.
$$
Retain only column indices in $1,\ldots,n$: the first or last row has no neighbor outside this range. Admissible repeated endpoint knots are obtained by a limit with zero spacings, provided $h_i+h_{i+1}>0$; a degenerate hat with zero support width is not a <basis> element.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The entries in (b) satisfy
$$
|g_{ii}|-\sum_{j\ne i}|g_{ij}|\ge\frac23-\frac{h_i+h_{i+1}}{3(h_i+h_{i+1})}=\frac13.
$$
Thus $G$ has <strict diagonal dominance> with margin at least $1/3$. To prove the <inverse infinity-norm bound from diagonal dominance> directly, choose $i$ with $|a_i|=\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}$. The <reverse triangle inequality> gives
$$
\|Ga\|_{\ell^\infty}\ge|(Ga)_i|\ge\left(|g_{ii}|-\sum_{j\ne i}|g_{ij}|\right)|a_i|\ge\frac13\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}.
$$
This also proves that the <kernel> is zero and hence that $G$ is invertible. Substituting $a=G^{-1}b$ and taking the <operator norm> gives
$$
\boxed{\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\le3}.
$$
Combined with (a), it yields $\|P_S\|_\infty\le3$ for linear <splines>, independently of the knot spacings. The diagonal-dominance argument already proves the required estimate.
